#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# dependencies = ["openpyxl"]
# ///
#
# openpyxl reads the shared sac-students.xlsx, which the other instructors edit
# in Teams and which therefore cannot be a .csv, and writes the workbook below.
# uv builds and caches the environment on first run, so the script is still
# started by name. See courseadmin.py's read_sac_students.
#
# test-admin — everything a test needs on paper, in one sitting
# Usage: test-admin N [--yes] [--dry-run] [--force] [--spare N] [--sac N]
#                     [--pages P] [--keep-tex] [--roster-dir DIR]
#                     [--stale-days DAYS]
#
#   N              the test number. A positive whole number; it names the outputs.
#   --yes          answer every question with its default, asking nothing. The
#                  steps done by hand are then listed, not asked about.
#   --dry-run      go through every step but write no files at all.
#   --force        replace an existing test-N.xlsx, losing its `Scanned' ticks.
#   --spare        extra booklets per section, without being asked (default:
#                  print-job.csv's spare_per_section)
#   --sac          booklets for the Student Accessibility Centre, the same number
#                  each day, without being asked (default: count
#                  sac-students.xlsx and add print-job.csv's sac_cushion)
#   --pages        pages in one booklet, for when the test PDFs cannot be
#                  measured (default: the page count of each day's test PDF)
#   --keep-tex     keep the generated attendance-sheet .tex after a good build.
#   --roster-dir   where the class-list exports are (default: the shared
#                  OneDrive folder, exported-classlists)
#   --stale-days   flag a class list older than this (default 21 -- the ask is
#                  an export every week or two)
#
# RUN ONCE PER TEST, a few days before it, when the test itself is finished. It
# walks through the job in the order it has to happen and stops to ask before
# each step that depends on a judgement:
#
#   1. THE TEST FILES   are both days' PDFs built, with solutions?        (ask)
#   2. THE CLASS LISTS  how old is each instructor's newest export?       (ask)
#   3. ENROLMENT        correct course-sections.csv from the class lists? (ask)
#   4. THE NUMBERS      spare booklets per section, and the SAC's block   (ask)
#   5. the attendance sheets are built
#   6. the workbook is written
#   7. THE FOLLOW-THROUGH  the steps done by hand, one at a time, each     (ask)
#      waiting for a `y': set up Crowdmark, label the questions, split its
#      PDFs, place both orders, drop the files, hand out the cover page
#
# and produces two files in the Team's Tests/ADMIN folder (courseadmin's
# OUTPUT_DIR):
#
#   test-N-attendance-sheet.pdf  one page per tutorial section, in the order
#                                course-sections.csv lists them, each with that
#                                section's students and a column to tick
#   test-N.xlsx                  four sheets, two per lecture day:
#                                  `Tue envelopes'  which booklets go in which
#                                  `Thu envelopes'  TA's envelope, with a box
#                                                   to tick once its tests have
#                                                   been scanned and uploaded
#                                  `Tue order'      the answers the Print
#                                  `Thu order'      Services web form asks for,
#                                                   one cell each
#
# THE NUMBERS ARE FIXED AT THE MOMENT OF THE RUN. A student or three may drop
# between the run and the test, and that is fine: the sheets, the envelopes and
# the order all describe the same stack of paper, and that agreement matters
# more than being current. So there is no "refresh" mode, and a rerun stops
# rather than overwrite a workbook whose boxes may already be ticked (--force).
#
# ONE ORDER PER LECTURE DAY. The course tutors on Tuesday and Thursday and a
# different version of the test is written on each, so there are two print jobs,
# two stacks of paper and two sets of envelopes. Everything is computed per day.
#
# WHY THE CLASS LISTS NEED A SCRIPT -- the registrar's export is awkward in one
# specific way. Downloading the class list for ANY ONE of an instructor's
# sections returns the students of ALL of that instructor's sections in a single
# file, with no section column. Which section a student is in is encoded like
# this:
#
#   "Last, First"                                    -> the section NAMED IN THE
#                                                       FILENAME
#   "Last, First cross-listed as MATH*1003*FR02A"    -> FR02A
#
# ("Cross-listed" is the registrar's word for it, not a real cross-listing.)
#
# So one download per instructor covers the whole course -- and THE FILENAME IS
# DATA. It is the only record of where the un-suffixed students belong, so a
# colleague who downloads their FR09A list rather than FR07A makes FR09A the
# bare-name section, and renaming a file casually would silently mis-assign every
# section that instructor teaches. That is why the downloads are meant to be
# dropped in UNRENAMED.
#
# THE FOLDER ACCUMULATES. Each instructor exports a fresh list every week or two
# and NOBODY DELETES ANYTHING -- the one instruction that stays reliable over a
# term, and the only safe one, since every alternative invites a rename. The
# filename also carries the export time, so this script takes the newest list per
# instructor and ignores the rest.
#
# THE ATTENDANCE SHEET IS BUILT IN THE REPO, DELIVERED TO THE TEAM. Its .tex is
# written to TESTS/ADMIN/ and compiled there with pr-latexmk, because the
# .latexmkrc that finds the machinery only works inside the course tree; the
# finished PDF is then moved out. The .tex is DELETED once the PDF is built and
# its page count verified: it is build residue, and a second plaintext copy of
# every student's name. It survives whenever that verification did not pass, and
# under --keep-tex, so it is still git-ignored. --keep-tex is for correcting a
# name or adding a late student by hand and recompiling.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACED. Two scripts, make-attendance-sheets and make-print-job,
# which were always run minutes apart and each needed telling what the other
# already knew. Before them, the ADMIN-10x3.xlsx `testN' sheets. Two deliberate
# differences from that spreadsheet survive:
#
#   SPARES.  It ordered enrolment + ROUNDUP(enrolment * 5%), so a 35-seat section
#            got two spares and a 16-seat section got one. A flat spare per
#            section is what is actually wanted.
#
#   THE SAC. Its "Special notes" read "set aside the **last** 11 tests (test #1
#            to test #11)" -- the parenthetical restarted its count at 1 and so
#            contradicted the word "last". Booklets are numbered continuously
#            here, SAC block included, and the note says the same thing once.
#
# PORTING TO ANOTHER COURSE. Nothing in these scripts is edited: they are shared,
# in the machinery submodule, and run as .course-machinery/admin/test-admin from
# the course's own checkout. Everything about the course's shape -- how many
# sections, their order, rooms, TAs, who teaches them -- is read from
# course-sections.csv, and section codes are not pattern-matched. (See the
# PORTING block below for the two things still assumed: the subject MATH, and
# Tuesday/Thursday tutorials.) The new course's course-info/ needs:
#
#   admin-settings.csv    columns setting, value: course_number and team_dir
#   course-sections.csv   columns Section, Enrolment, Day, Room, Instructor, TA
#   print-job.csv         columns field, label, value  (see read_print_job)
#   calendar-dates.csv    columns week, date, id, tutorial  (optional)
#   courseinfo.sty        for \coursenum in the sheet's heading
#
# courseadmin.py, checkbox_xlsx.py and the sheet's layout, attendance.sty, sit
# beside this script; the last is found by LaTeX on TEXINPUTS.
#
# plus a TESTS/ADMIN/ in that repo to build in (holding a .latexmkrc), a
# .MIRRORDIR at the repo's top level saying where built PDFs go, pr-latexmk and
# pdfinfo on PATH, a Team folder for the output, and .gitignore entries for the
# class lists and the generated test*-attendance-sheet.tex, both of which hold
# student names. Write those entries UNANCHORED: they guard against a file
# appearing rather than tracking one that exists, so a path-anchored pattern
# stops guarding the next time the tree is rearranged, and does it silently.

